    "excerpt": "A lot of parents wait until something seems wrong before scheduling a dental visit for their child. In most cases, that is later than ideal.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-03-14",
    "readTimeMinutes": 6,
    "markdown": "A lot of parents wait until something seems wrong before scheduling a dental visit for their child. In most cases, that is later than ideal.\n\nThe better move is to start early, before pain, fear, or visible problems show up. Early dental visits help children get comfortable with the office, help parents learn how to care for baby teeth, and give your dentist a chance to catch small issues before they become stressful ones.\n\nAt Family First Smile Care, that early, family-centered approach fits the heart of the practice. The office emphasizes gentle care, prevention, and a calm experience for children and anxious patients alike. For Los Gatos families, that is exactly what a first visit should feel like.\n\n## So when should kids first see a dentist?\n\nA good rule of thumb is this: your child should see a dentist within 6 months of the first tooth coming in, or by the first birthday at the latest.\n\nThat recommendation is consistent with current child oral health guidance and is also reinforced by Stanford Medicine Children's Health. In its first dental visit fact sheet, Stanford notes that experts recommend a child's first dental visit within 6 months of the first tooth eruption or by about 12 months of age.\n\nThat timing surprises a lot of parents. If your baby only has one or two tiny teeth, it may feel too early. But baby teeth matter right away. They affect eating, speech development, spacing for adult teeth, and overall oral health habits.\n\n## Why the first visit should happen early\n\nThe first visit is not usually about major treatment. It is mostly about prevention, education, and comfort.\n\nStarting early can help with:\n\n- Checking for early signs of decay\n- Talking through feeding habits and cavity risk\n- Guidance on brushing baby teeth and cleaning gums\n- Questions about pacifiers, thumb-sucking, or teething\n- Helping your child see the dental office as normal and safe\n\nThat last point matters more than people think. A child whose first visit happens because of pain or an emergency may build anxiety much faster than a child whose first visit is calm and low pressure.\n\n## What happens at a child's first dental visit?\n\nFor many children, the first appointment is short, gentle, and very parent-involved.\n\nDepending on your child's age and comfort level, the visit may include:\n\n- A quick exam of the teeth, gums, bite, and oral tissues\n- A conversation about growth and development\n- Tips for brushing and fluoride use at home\n- Guidance on snacks, drinks, and bottle habits\n- A gentle cleaning if appropriate\n\nStanford's guidance also notes that the first visit often lasts about 30 to 45 minutes and is designed in part to help the child feel comfortable with the dentist. That is a useful expectation for parents. The goal is not perfection. The goal is a positive foundation.\n\n## How Los Gatos parents can help kids feel comfortable\n\nOne of the most helpful things parents can do is stay calm themselves.\n\nChildren read adult energy fast. If a parent acts tense, apologizes over and over, or talks about the dentist like something scary is coming, kids pick up on that. Stanford's first visit guidance specifically advises parents not to pass their own dental anxiety to their child.\n\nA few practical tips:\n\n- Book an appointment when your child is usually rested and fed\n- Use simple, neutral language about the visit\n- Avoid saying things like \"it will not hurt\" before anyone has asked\n- Bring comfort items if that helps your child settle\n- Let the dental team guide the pace when possible\n\nFamily First Smile Care's emphasis on compassionate, patient-centered care makes that kind of visit easier. For many parents, choosing the right office is half the battle.\n\n## What if my child is nervous, strong-willed, or very young?\n\nThat is common. It does not mean you should wait.\n\nSome children cry. Some cling to a parent. Some refuse to open wide right away. None of that is unusual, especially in toddlers. What matters is working with a practice that expects child behavior to be child behavior and responds with patience instead of pressure.\n\nStanford's guidance also points out that children respond differently at different ages. Very young children may struggle with separation. Preschoolers may be unpredictable. That is normal developmental behavior, not a sign that the visit was a mistake.\n\n## Why baby teeth deserve real attention\n\nSome parents still hear the old idea that baby teeth do not matter because they fall out anyway. That is not true.\n\nBaby teeth help children chew comfortably, speak clearly, and hold space for permanent teeth. Cavities in baby teeth can still cause pain, infection, sleep problems, and trouble eating. They can also create negative feelings around dental care if the first experience becomes urgent.\n\nAn early dental home helps prevent that.\n\n## What parents should ask at the first visit\n\nYour first appointment is also your chance to get clear answers. Good questions include:\n\n- Is my child's brushing routine appropriate for their age?\n- How much fluoride toothpaste should we use?\n- Are there feeding habits increasing cavity risk?\n- Is thumb-sucking or pacifier use becoming an issue?\n- When should we schedule the next exam?\n\nIf your child tends to be anxious, stubborn, or especially sensitive in new environments, share that too. The more context your dentist has, the better they can shape the visit.\n\n## How often should kids go after the first visit?\n\nFor many children, checkups every 6 months are a good baseline. Some kids may need more frequent visits depending on cavity risk, enamel concerns, or developmental issues.\n\nThe important thing is consistency. Regular visits help dental care feel routine instead of dramatic.\n\n## Signs you should not wait for the first birthday\n\nIf your child already has teeth and you notice any of the following, schedule a visit sooner:\n\n- White or brown spots on teeth\n- Ongoing sensitivity to brushing\n- Trouble eating because of mouth discomfort\n- Swelling or unusual gum changes\n- Dental trauma from a fall or bump\n\nEven if nothing looks wrong, though, it is still worth establishing care early.\n\n## FAQ\n\n### Is age one really not too early for a dentist?\nNo. Age one is the recommended limit, and many children should be seen earlier if teeth have already erupted.\n\n### What if my child cries at the first visit?\nThat is common. A calm, gentle visit can still be successful even if your child is unsure at first.\n\n### Do baby teeth really need checkups?\nYes. Baby teeth affect comfort, eating, speech, and the spacing of future adult teeth.\n\n### How can I prepare my child for the first dental visit?\nKeep the explanation simple, choose a good time of day, and stay calm. Your energy helps shape the experience.\n\nIf you are wondering when kids should first see a dentist, the safest answer is usually earlier than most parents expect. Family First Smile Care helps Los Gatos families start with gentle, prevention-focused visits that build healthy smiles and positive dental habits from the very beginning.",
